WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
VOICE OVER: You may remember staining slides&nbsp;
in biology class, dipping them in red and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:11.760
purple dyes to reveal microscopic structures.&nbsp;
Pathologists use similar techniques to diagnose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.760
diseases like cancer. But now researchers have&nbsp;
developed a new type of microscope slide that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:30.160
uses nanotechnology to change the colour of cells&nbsp;
and reveal cancers without any staining at all

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.200
The slides, called nano-m-slides, work&nbsp;
using electrons embedded on their surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:38.400
that wiggle or oscillate when&nbsp;
they're hit with a beam of light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:44.240
As light from a microscope passes through a&nbsp;
piece of tissue the specific characteristics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.280
of that tissue cause the electrons to react&nbsp;
in different ways and that changes the colour.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:55.680
BRIAN ABBEY: We create a pattern across the entire&nbsp;
slide which basically makes the surface into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:00.720
a type of sensor, so your entire&nbsp;
microscope slide then becomes a sensor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.760
and depending on what gets put on that sensor&nbsp;
different wavelengths of the ambient light are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:15.280
absorbed. So if, for example, I put a healthy&nbsp;
cell on on our sensor it absorbs a certain set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:20.000
of wavelengths and if you take a cancer&nbsp;
cell and you place it on this this sensor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.040
it will absorb a different set of wavelengths&nbsp;
because its composition is slightly different.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.800
V/O: The researchers aren't entirely&nbsp;
sure what it is about the cancer cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.000
that makes them show up in different colours. It&nbsp;
could be the density of proteins within the cell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:39.840
something about the cell skeleton or the way its&nbsp;
contents are organised. In this example normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.960
cells turn yellow and breast cancer cells&nbsp;
turn green. And that could allow for quick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:53.840
accurate diagnoses that could be vital in&nbsp;
the treatment of diseases like breast cancer

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.000
BELINDA PARKER: More and more women are&nbsp;
being diagnosed with really early stages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:03.120
breast cancer, for example. More women are&nbsp;
getting biopsies, to really look - do they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:07.840
actually have cancer? Or, you know, an&nbsp;
earlier stage or a benign abnormality.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.680
V/O:In a standard cancer screen -&nbsp;
biopsy tissue taken from the breast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.720
must be embedded in wax, sliced very&nbsp;
thin, then placed on a slide and stained.

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:23.840
BELINDA PARKER: Usually a pathologist may be&nbsp;
looking at a subtle change in the shape of a cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.960
amongst a tissue of thousands or millions of cells&nbsp;
- and, you know, this nanoslide was really picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.400
up a difference in a cancer cell compared to&nbsp;
another early, sort of, change in these cells.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.320
V/O: By eliminating the staining&nbsp;
step, the nano-M-slides produced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:43.360
clear and accurate results&nbsp;
in as little as 10 minutes.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:47.920
BELINDA PARKER: It was quite amazing&nbsp;
that we could just see these cancer cells&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.600
through a conventional microscope, so a normal&nbsp;
microscope would really distinguish these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.520
cancer cells from from other surrounding cells -&nbsp;
including the very cells that cancer arises from.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:04.320
V/O: Nano-M-slides probably won't be a&nbsp;
replacement for other diagnostic methods&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.680
but they could provide one&nbsp;
more tool to help pathologists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:12.000
standardise their diagnosis of diseases,&nbsp;
which can sometimes be difficult to call.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.640
BRIAN ABBEY: We see that this work as really&nbsp;
being the tip of the iceberg. There are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:23.440
other disease states and other cancers, in fact,&nbsp;
that we are also pursuing as well in the meantime.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:27.920
V/O: The researchers say that knowing&nbsp;
immediately whether a tissue is cancerous or not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:31.840
could help surgeons decide which&nbsp;
sections of a tumour to remove.

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:35.600
BRIAN ABBEY: It's time critical you know, the&nbsp;
patient's lying on the table you need to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:39.680
a - the surgeon and the pathologist&nbsp;
wants to get a - result as quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:42.960
as possible to minimise the amount&nbsp;
of time that the patient is under.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.560
And these slides, because of the fact that&nbsp;
they don't require any chemistry in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:57.920
of the standing or labelling, could have a huge&nbsp;
positive impact, I think, in that in that setting.

